 "Ask a shopkeeper how a price was decided and the answer usually sounds more solid than it is. The oldest method, called cost-plus pricing, simply adds a fixed margin onto whatever the item cost to make, which feels fair and is easy to explain to a shareholder. Economists have long pointed out a problem with it: cost has very little to do with what a customer is willing to pay. A hotel room and a bottle of water cost roughly the same to provide on a scorching afternoon as on a quiet one, yet almost nobody prices them identically, because what the customer is actually paying for is availability at the moment they need it, not the object itself.",
 "The alternative, broadly called value-based pricing, starts from the customer rather than the factory. A piece of software might cost almost nothing extra to sell to one more customer, so its price has almost no relationship to production cost at all; instead, a company tries to estimate how much time or money the software saves the buyer, and prices somewhere below that figure. Reaching that estimate usually involves testing: showing slightly different prices to different groups of customers online and watching which figure sells the most without denting revenue. Airlines and hotels take the logic furthest, changing a price several times a day as a flight fills up, on the theory that the last seat sold is worth far more than the first.",
