 "By the time most of the city is having breakfast, the wholesale market on its outskirts has already finished its busiest hours and is being hosed down for the day. Lorries loaded overnight at farms and fishing ports across the region begin arriving from around midnight, and by three or four in the morning the aisles are crowded with restaurant owners, small grocers and hotel buyers picking through crates of produce that in some cases left the ground or the water less than a day earlier. Trading is largely finished by seven, well before the office workers who will eventually eat the food have left home, which means the market runs almost entirely on a clock that the rest of the city never sees.",
