 "The idea behind the barcode is often traced to a beach in Florida in 1948, where a graduate student named Norman Joseph Woodland is said to have dragged his fingers through the sand, thinking about a supermarket manager's request for a faster way to record what customers bought. He and a colleague filed a patent the following year for a set of thick and thin lines, inspired partly by the dots and dashes of Morse code, that could be read by a beam of light and translated into a number. The idea was sound, but the technology of the early 1950s was not: the scanner they built required an intensely bright light and a bulky vacuum-tube reader, far too expensive and impractical for any ordinary shop to install.",
 "For roughly two decades the patent sat largely unused, because reading a barcode reliably and cheaply required two things that did not yet exist together: a compact laser and a computer inexpensive enough for a supermarket to own. Both arrived by the early 1970s, and a committee representing American grocery manufacturers and retailers spent several years arguing over a single, crucial decision: which barcode design every company in the industry would agree to use, since a code readable by only one chain's scanners would be worthless to everyone else. The committee settled on the Universal Product Code in 1973, a format still recognisable on packaging today, and manufacturers were asked to begin printing it on their products even before a single working scanner had been installed in a shop.",
 "The first product scanned at a checkout using the new code was, by most accounts, a packet of chewing gum, passed over a scanner in an Ohio supermarket on 26 June 1974; that packet is now kept in a museum as a small piece of retail history. Adoption afterwards was gradual rather than sudden, since installing a scanner at every checkout remained expensive well into the 1980s, and many smaller shops continued ringing up prices by hand for years after the technology existed. What eventually tipped the balance was not the scanner alone but the falling cost of the computers behind it, letting a store link sales directly to its stock records for the first time.",
